# Overview
#
# Each phrase wraps a behavior in its purpose or duty (`the hook is
# designed to run after every write`). The sentence then says what the
# hook is for, and leaves the reader to infer what it does. The fix
# gives the verb the subject (`the hook runs after every write`). When
# the phrase states a duty that binds a person, the fix grades it with
# `must` or `should`, as the technical-writing skill asks (`the caller
# is responsible for closing the body` becomes `the caller must close
# the body`). The rule sits at Recommended, because a phrase that names
# a goal the subject might not reach also matches.
#
# Expression
#
# `main` lists each phrase in its singular and its plural form (`is
# designed to`, `are designed to`). `exists to` and `the purpose of ...
# is to` wrap a behavior the same way (`the flag exists to skip the
# cache` becomes `the flag skips the cache`).
#
# In a pattern, `*` matches one word. A list picks its longest match and
# never backtracks, so each gap length is its own alternative, and the
# purpose phrase appears twice. One form takes a one-word noun after
# `of`, and the other takes a two-word one (`the goal of this flag is
# to`).
#
# False Positives
#
# `is meant for` stays out, because it names a use (`the flag is meant
# for testing`).
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 150 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 18 of 20 sampled
# hits read better with the behavior as the verb or with `must`. The 2
# misfires named a goal that the subject might not reach (`the project
# aims to not break compatibility`).
expr main [(is designed to), (are designed to), (is intended to), (are intended to), (is meant to), (are meant to), (is responsible for), (are responsible for), (aims to), (aim to), (serves to), (serve to), (exists to), (exist to), (the [purpose, goal] of * [is, was] to), (the [purpose, goal] of * * [is, was] to)]
let message "Intent phrase. State what the subject does, with the behavior as its verb, or write `must` or `should` for a person's duty."
let description "Flags phrases that wrap a behavior in its purpose or duty."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The caller closes the handle after the last read."
allows "The flag is meant for testing."
